  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Domain Logic**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the user\'s domain logic, specifically concerning music key normalization and the Cifra Club transposition feature (`?tom={N}`). My initial analysis centers around deciphering the `originalKey` input, handling various textual representations (e.g., \"C\", \"D\303\263\", \"Am\", \"Sol Maior\", etc.). I\'m working to extract this input\'s music key, and figuring out how that key translates to Cifra Club parameters.\n\n\n**Defining Key Conversion**\n\nI\'ve been working on a function that translates music key strings (like \"D\303\263\", \"A#\", or \"Gb\") into a numerical note index (0-11). This index helps to map the key to a canonical note name (C, C#, D, etc.). I\'m particularly focused on handling the various textual representations users might input.\n\n\n**Constructing URL Slugs**\n\nI\'m now focusing on automatic slug generation for artists and songs. I\'ve defined the rules: artist names go lowercase, accents are removed, and non-alphanumeric characters become hyphens, with extra hyphens trimmed. The song slug follows similar rules. The standard URL will be `https://www.cifraclub.com.br/{slug-artista}/{slug-musica}/`. I\'m also considering preview options and custom slug overrides.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Coloring Theme Badges**\n\nI\'m designing a system for theme badges. My goal is to assign a unique, vibrant, and accessible color to each theme text (like \"Pagode 90\"). I\'ll compute a hash integer from the theme name and map it to a predefined color palette (e.g., emerald, rose, sky). This ensures consistent background and text colors for each theme across the application.\n\n\n**Defining Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining data structures. I\'m building out the schema for entities like `BlockItem`, `Block`, and `Setlist`, including the relationships between them. I am paying close attention to data types, required fields, and optional fields. I am thinking carefully about key assignments like `originalKeyAtAssignment` in `BlockItem` to ensure data integrity and track changes, as well as the different setlist permissions and roles for shared setlists.\n\n\n**Designing Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve been refining the data structures for Setlist entities. I am focusing on modeling the relationships between `Setlist`, `User`, and `Invitation`, with the addition of `Block` data. I\'m building out a schema with fields such as `ownerEmail`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `blocks`, and `members`. I\'m paying close attention to data types and ensuring efficient relationships for storing and retrieving setlist data. I am also planning data seeding for initial login, to display realistic setlists. I am prioritizing multi-user simulation with email login and invitation support.\n\n\n**Planning Code Structure**\n\nI am now focusing on organizing the codebase for setlist features. I am defining modules for types, utilities (key normalization, URL generation), storage (local and Supabase integration), state management (Zustand store), and UI components (header, bottom navigation, modals, toasts). I am focusing on clear separation of concerns, and efficient data handling. I\'m also planning for cascade deletions with confirmation modals, undo toasts (with a 5-second timer), search, and an in-app webview for displaying cifras.\n\n\n**Structuring UI Components**\n\nI\'m now focusing on UI components, specifically for setlists and catalogs. I\'m fleshing out components like `ConfirmModal` (double confirmation with summaries), `CifraWebviewModal` (in-app webview), and the views: `SetlistsList`, `SetlistDetail`, `FocusedBlockView`, `CatalogView`, and `ProfileView`, including pending invitations and dark mode.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 355274904
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
