  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining ID Structures**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of ID implementation. I\'ve considered local IDs as strings, such as \'song\\_01\', \'set\\_01\', and UUIDs or base-36 random strings. I\'m currently assessing the implications of using these arbitrary strings and the potential insertion problems that may arise.\n\n\n**Refining ID Conversion Strategy**\n\nI\'ve realized the direct mapping of string IDs to Supabase UUID columns will fail. My solution is to detect and handle string IDs by converting them to valid UUIDs. I\'ll use a deterministic UUID v4 generator helper function for consistency, or generate new IDs using `crypto.randomUUID()`. I am now inspecting the user\'s Supabase schema, focusing on `profiles`, `songs`, `setlists`, and `blocks` tables, paying close attention to the `id` and foreign key relationships that are defined as `uuid` columns.\n\n\n**Analyzing Schema Relationships**\n\nI\'m now examining the foreign key relationships within the schema to understand data integrity. Specifically, I\'m reviewing how `setlists` connect to `block_songs`, `setlist_members`, and `setlist_invites`. I see that the schema uses UUIDs consistently for IDs and foreign keys, which is good. Also, after reviewing the schema, I note the RLS policies, specifically the `songs` table with a policy `Users can view own songs` using `auth.uid() = user_id`.\n\n\n**Investigating RLS Impacts**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the implications of Supabase\'s Row Level Security (RLS) policies, especially with unauthenticated requests. I\'ve realized that using the Supabase JS client with anonKey, `auth.uid()` is null, which causes RLS policies like `auth.uid() = user_id` to block data access. Running `testSupabaseConnection()` with RLS enabled returns `success: true` despite restricted access! This has implications for `syncLocalDataToSupabase` and `fetchRemoteDataFromSupabase`. Also, I\'m thinking of how to deterministically convert arbitrary string IDs into UUIDs in `src/lib/supabase.ts`.\n\n\n**Validating UUID Generation**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that the Supabase `uuid` column readily accepts both genuine UUIDs and the converted strings. I\'ve tested a clean deterministic conversion of arbitrary local string IDs into valid UUIDs. My generated function provides a reliable, consistent method to ensure seamless integration with Supabase, which ensures that local and remote IDs are kept in sync. This is critical for model consistency.\n\n\n**Mapping Table Attributes**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on precisely how the data from `Item`, `CatalogSong`, and related objects will map to database columns. I\'m writing down the mapping for each table, including `songs`, `setlists`, `blocks`, `block_songs`, and `setlist_members`. Each table\'s fields are clearly laid out with their corresponding source fields, including how I\'ll handle potential null values or missing data. This includes how I plan to handle deterministic UUID generation using the `toUUID()` helper.\n\n\n**Defining Data Retrieval Process**\n\nI\'m now detailing the data retrieval process from Supabase, focusing on reconstructing local `Setlist` objects. I\'m carefully outlining the retrieval and assembly of data from `songs`, `setlists`, `blocks`, `block_songs`, and `setlist_members` tables. I\'m paying special attention to the sorting of `blocks` and `block_songs` by `position`. I\'m building a plan to handle the nested structure to create a full `Setlist` object with its nested components.\n\n\n**Confirming Bidirectional Sync**\n\nI\'ve meticulously reviewed the data retrieval steps, confirming they perfectly align with the Supabase schema and guarantee full bidirectional synchronization of setlist data.  I\'ve also determined the `SUPABASE_SQL_SCHEMA` export in `src/lib/supabase.ts` must reflect the user\'s exact SQL schema for consistency.\n\n\n"
